VERDICT
Correct: your code correctly converts each number to a string to count its characters, accurately identifying numbers with an even number of digits.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well:

You initialized your counter count to 0 and returned it properly.

You used a simple loop that visits every number in the nums array.

You correctly checked parity with (digi_count % 2 == 0) to spot even digit lengths.

Is it correct?

Yes, it is fully correct and passes all test cases on LeetCode.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses:

There are no logical bugs under LeetCode's constraints (1 <= nums[i] <= 10^5).

Converting integers to strings via to_string() creates extra heap/string buffer allocations, which is slower than using pure arithmetic.

Edge cases handled properly: single-digit numbers (odd length 1, skipped), maximum value in constraints (100,000 has 6 digits, counted), and arrays where all or none of the numbers have even digits.

Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason:

Time complexity: O(n * d) where n is the number of elements and d is the average number of digits (at most 6 here), because each number is converted into a string character by character.

Space complexity: O(d) auxiliary space because to_string creates a temporary string object of up to d characters for each number.

Why my approach works:

Take nums = [12, 345, 2]. 12 converts to "12", length is 2 (even), so count becomes 1. 345 converts to "345", length is 3 (odd). 2 converts to "2", length is 1 (odd). The function finishes and returns 1.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: String Conversion (Your Approach)

Where it comes from:

Canonical beginner approach found in introductory programming tutorials and LeetCode community discussions.

Idea:

Turn each integer into a string of characters using std::to_string, check if the string's length is even, and increment the counter.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <vector>
#include <string>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int findNumbers(vector<int>& nums) {
        int count = 0;
        for (int num : nums) {
            string s = to_string(num);
            if (s.length() % 2 == 0) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n * d) where n is the array length and d is the number of digits, as each integer is converted to text.

Space Complexity:

O(d) auxiliary space per number to allocate and store the string buffer.

What makes the next approach better:

Converting numbers to strings involves dynamic memory allocations. We can count digits in-place using standard division by 10 without any string overhead.

Approach 2: Repeated Division by 10 (Mathematical Digit Counting)

Where it comes from:

Official LeetCode editorial and classic textbook digit extraction.

Idea:

For each number, divide it by 10 in a while loop until it becomes 0, counting how many divisions took place. If that count is even, increment the main answer.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int findNumbers(vector<int>& nums) {
        int count = 0;
        for (int num : nums) {
            int digits = 0;
            while (num > 0) {
                digits++;
                num /= 10;
            }
            if (digits % 2 == 0) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n * log10(m)) where m is the maximum number in nums, since dividing by 10 processes one digit per step.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space because it only uses primitive integer counters.

What makes the next approach better:

Instead of using a loop to count digits, we can find the number of digits directly in one mathematical calculation using base-10 logarithms.

Approach 3: Base-10 Logarithm (Math Formula)

Where it comes from:

LeetCode editorial Approach 2 and standard mathematical number theory.

Idea:

The number of digits in a positive integer num is floor(log10(num)) + 1. For example, log10(12) is roughly 1.079; floor gives 1, plus 1 gives 2 digits. We can check if this digit count is even.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <vector>
#include <cmath>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int findNumbers(vector<int>& nums) {
        int count = 0;
        for (int num : nums) {
            int digits = floor(log10(num)) + 1;
            if (digits % 2 == 0) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) because floor(log10(num)) runs in constant O(1) time per number on modern CPU math coprocessors.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space.

What makes the next approach better:

Floating-point log10 and floor functions carry minor precision and speed overhead compared to simple integer comparisons. Given the problem constraints, we can check the exact intervals directly.

Approach 4: Constraint-Based Range Checks (Fastest & Branchless/Direct)

Where it comes from:

Top-voted LeetCode discussion solutions and competitive programming optimizations.

Idea:

The problem constraints specify 1 <= nums[i] <= 100,000 (10^5). A number has an even number of digits only if it has 2 digits (10 to 99), 4 digits (1,000 to 9,999), or 6 digits (100,000). We can check these three ranges directly with basic comparisons.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int findNumbers(vector<int>& nums) {
        int count = 0;
        for (int num : nums) {
            if ((num >= 10 && num <= 99) || 
                (num >= 1000 && num <= 9999) || 
                (num == 100000)) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) since each number is evaluated with at most 3 integer range comparisons in O(1) time.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use?

You used Approach 1 (String Conversion).

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem?

Interviewers almost always forbid converting integers to strings and expect Approach 2 (Repeated Division by 10) or Approach 3 (Base-10 Logarithm). They want to see that you can work with digits using math. Mentioning Approach 4 shows great attention to problem constraints.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:

Question 1: What if nums contains 0 or negative numbers?

Answer: For 0, log10(0) is mathematically undefined (returns -infinity or domain error), and the while (num > 0) loop would report 0 digits unless handled specifically as 1 digit. For negative numbers, take std::abs(num) first since a negative sign does not count as a digit.

Question 2: Why is floor(log10(num)) + 1 equal to the number of digits?

Answer: In base 10, every additional digit multiplies the magnitude by 10. Taking log10 gives the power of 10 that produces the number; adding 1 and flooring accounts for the 0-indexed exponent range (e.g., 10^1 to 10^2 - 1 all have log10 in [1, 2)).

Question 3: Can we count digits in base 2 (binary) or base 16 (hexadecimal)?

Answer: Yes. For base 2, replace division by 10 with division by 2 (or bit-shift num >>= 1), or use log2 / CPU built-ins like 32 - __builtin_clz(num).

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence:

You can count the digits of a number by repeatedly dividing by 10, by using floor(log10(num)) + 1, or by checking value ranges based on constraints.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time:

Avoid converting numbers to strings when pure arithmetic can do the job without memory allocations.

Use range-based for loops (for (int num : nums)) instead of indexing for cleaner code.

Always verify what happens if a number is 0 before using division loops or log10.

The pattern or idea this question is really about:

Digit Extraction, Modulo/Division Arithmetic, and Logarithmic Math.

